  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
